  "lesson": {
    "status": "published",
    "title": {
      "en": "Resistors in series and parallel, heating effect and power"
    },
    "blocks": [
      {
        "id": "story",
        "type": "story",
        "provenance": "original",
        "title": {
          "en": "Fuses and home wiring"
        },
        "text": {
          "en": "Why do all lights in a house work independently? Because home circuits use parallel connection. We also study how current heats wires and how power is calculated."
        }
      },
      {
        "id": "explain",
        "type": "explanation",
        "provenance": "original",
        "keepEnglishTerms": [
          "1 kWh = 3.6 x 10^6 J",
          "H = I^2 R t",
          "P = VI",
          "fuse",
          "home wiring",
          "parallel: 1/R",
          "series: R = R1 + R2",
          "tungsten filament"
        ],
        "text": {
          "en": "Resistors in series: the same current flows through each, the total R = R1 + R2 + R3 and the voltage is divided.\nResistors in parallel: the voltage is the same across each, the current is divided and 1/R = 1/R1 + 1/R2 + 1/R3.\nParallel resistance is smaller than the smallest resistor, which is why parallel connection is used in homes: each appliance works independently.\nHeating effect of current (Joule's law): H = I squared R t. Heaters, toasters and electric irons use this effect.\nA fuse has a low melting point wire in series. If the current is too high, it melts and breaks the circuit.\nElectric power P = VI = I squared R = V squared / R. The unit is the watt.\nElectrical energy = power x time. The commercial unit is the kilowatt-hour: 1 kWh = 3.6 x 10^6 J.\nBulbs have tungsten filaments because tungsten has a high melting point."
        }
      },
